            slug: 'chod',
            title: 'Chöd',
            native: 'གཅོད་',
            translit: 'gcod',
            summary: 'Machig Labdrön\'s \'cutting\' — a contemplative practice that stages, in imagination and in the most frightening place available, the giving away of the one thing a person is least willing to give.',
            paragraphs: [
                'It is best to say at the outset what is being cut. Gcod means \'cutting\', and its object is bdag \'dzin, self-clinging — the reflex by which a person grasps at a self and defends it. The practice is visionary and contemplative from beginning to end. Nothing whatever is done to the body; the offering is made in imagination, within a liturgy, and the tradition\'s own literature is unambiguous that a practitioner who took it literally would have inverted the entire meaning of the rite, since the point is the release of attachment and not the infliction of harm on anyone, oneself included. Machig\'s own explanation identifies the true objects as the four demons, bdud — the tangible, the intangible, the demon of exhilaration, and the fourth and root of the others, \'the inflater\', that steady low reflex of self-importance which makes the first three possible. These are named as demons, and they are described as states of mind.',
                'Machig Labdrön (traditionally 1055–1149) was a Tibetan woman, a prodigy reciter of the Perfection of Wisdom sūtras who left monastic life, bore children, and studied with the Indian master Padampa Sangye, whose Zhijé or \'Pacification\' transmission stands behind her work, and with Kyotön Sönam Lama. She is credited as the originator of a lineage — and the traditional accounts hold that Indian scholars travelled to Tibet to examine her teaching, found it sound, and carried it back into India, which would make chöd the rare case of a transmission running from Tibet to the source rather than from it. Historians, Michelle Sorensen among them, read that episode as at least partly a claim to legitimacy, since Tibetan traditions ordinarily authenticated themselves by demonstrating Indian origin and this one could not. What is not in dispute is that chöd took its distinctive shape in Tibet, and that a Tibetan woman gave it that shape. The Blue Annals of 1476–78 gives it a chapter alongside Zhijé.',
                'Chöd is classified not as a tantra but as a practice of the Perfection of Wisdom, and its own texts describe it as the Prajñāpāramitā put into action. The logic is exact and rather beautiful. If the aggregates are empty, then the body offered is empty; the guests who receive it are empty; and the fear that rises to resist the offering is the last thing holding the whole apparatus of self in place — which makes fear not an obstacle to the practice but its instrument and its evidence. The visualised feast is enumerated by its guests: the enlightened ones above, the protectors, the beings of the six realms, and the karmic creditors, those to whom one is held to owe a debt. It is distributed as a \'white\' and a \'red\' offering. Every element of it takes place in the mind of the person singing.',
                'The setting is the charnel ground, dur khrod, and the hagiographies describe it as sought deliberately: cremation grounds, sky-burial sites, high passes, and places with a local reputation for being dangerous, entered alone and at night. This is a laboratory rather than a theatre — the frightening place is there to produce a reaction that can then be examined, and the tradition is entirely candid that the reaction is the material. The liturgy is sung, and the practitioner accompanies it on the ḍamaru, a double-headed hand-drum turned by a twist of the wrist, and the rkang gling, a short trumpet traditionally of human thighbone, with a bell. Bone instruments belong to the same charnel-ground symbolism as the setting: they are reminders of mortality made audible, and their use was regulated by monastic and local custom. Instruments of other materials are common today.',
                'Chöd is as much a musical form as a doctrinal one, and this is why it travelled. Its melodies, dbyangs, are distinctive and were transmitted as carefully as the words; a chöd liturgy is sung to a known tune, at a known tempo, with the drum keeping it. Itinerant chödpa were a familiar sight across the plateau and through the Himalaya, often summoned to households or villages visited by epidemic or misfortune, which placed the practice in a social role somewhere between contemplative discipline and public service. Several of its transmission lines came close to extinction, and in the nineteenth century Jamgön Kongtrul gathered what survived into the chöd section of his Treasury of Instructions — the reason a good deal of it can be studied at all. It is held today by every school of Tibetan Buddhism and by Bön, one of the few things on which all of them agree.',
            ],
