            slug: 'treasure-texts',
            title: 'Treasure Texts',
            native: 'གཏེར་མ་',
            translit: 'gter ma',
            summary: 'A revelation system holding that teachings concealed in the eighth century surface when the age requires them — and the channel through which a tradition that grounded all authority in antiquity could nonetheless go on producing new scripture.',
            paragraphs: [
                'The account runs as follows. Padmasambhava, in the eighth century, foresaw that certain teachings were unsuited to his own age and would be needed in ages to come, and concealed them accordingly, appointing his close students to be reborn at the appointed times as their finders. Yeshe Tsogyal, his Tibetan consort and, in the tradition\'s telling, a woman of prodigious memory who could retain a teaching on a single hearing, wrote them down and performed much of the concealing. The hiding places are various to the point of extravagance: rock faces, lakes, statues, temple pillars, the sky itself, and — most consequentially — the mindstreams of the future revealers, where a teaching could be lodged directly and left to ripen across lifetimes.',
                'Hence the two principal kinds. Sa gter, earth treasure, is a physical object recovered from a physical place: characteristically a shog ser, a small yellow scroll, but also statues, ritual implements and compounded medicines. Dgongs gter, mind treasure, arises directly in the revealer\'s awareness with no material substrate at all. Two further categories are recognised: pure vision, dag snang, and yang gter, the re-revelation of something already revealed and subsequently lost. The scrolls are described as written in mkha\' \'gro brda yig, the symbolic script of the ḍākinīs — not a language so much as a cipher of signs, sometimes a single syllable, which the destined revealer alone can unfold into volumes and which is illegible to everyone else. The claim is, by design, one that cannot be checked from outside, and the tradition understood this perfectly well. It is exactly why the machinery of authentication mattered so much to it.',
                'The gter ston are numbered in the hundreds; the classic roll of the great ones runs to a hundred and eight. Nyangral Nyima Özer (1124–1192) and Guru Chöwang (1212–1270) stand at the head of the line. Orgyen Lingpa\'s Padma Kathang gave Tibet its standard life of Padmasambhava and, with it, much of the country\'s picture of its own conversion. Jigme Lingpa\'s Longchen Nyingtik, an eighteenth-century mind treasure received in visions of Longchenpa, became the most widely practised Nyingma cycle in existence. In the nineteenth century Jamgön Kongtrul gathered what survived into the Rinchen Terdzö, some sixty-three volumes in its Palpung edition and more in later printings, an act of preservation without which a great deal would simply have gone. The school\'s older tantras sit separately in the Nyingma Gyübum, a canon running parallel to the Kangyur rather than inside it.',
                'Terma was contested in Tibet centuries before any foreign scholar took an interest, and the objections were philological rather than pious. Butön, ordering the canon in the fourteenth century, excluded most Nyingma tantras on the straightforward ground that no Indic originals could be produced for them. Chag Lotsāwa Chöjé Pal (1197–1264) wrote against texts he judged fabricated. Drigung Paldzin attacked the Nyingma corpus directly in a treatise distinguishing Dharma from what he held was not, and Sakya Paṇḍita was characteristically sharp about revelation claims generally. The tradition answered with criteria rather than indignation: prophetic certificates — the kha byang or inventory and the lung byang or prophetic guide — naming the revealer, the place and the time in advance; corroboration between independent revealers of the same cycle; the doctrinal coherence of the recovered material with what was already accepted. And it answered with a counter-question — that its critics accepted the Indian pedigree of their own scriptures on evidence not obviously stronger.',
                'Modern scholarship, Janet Gyatso\'s above all, has largely moved past the question of forgery to a more interesting one: how a culture that grounded all religious authority in antiquity nonetheless built a legitimate channel for the genuinely new. Terma is that channel, and it worked. It allowed Tibetan Buddhism to keep composing while sincerely insisting that it was only remembering, and it conferred authority on visionaries standing well outside the monastic establishment — laymen, married yogins, itinerants, and on occasion women. It is worth adding, against any temptation to read the institution cynically, that the Fifth Dalai Lama, the most powerful figure Tibet ever produced and no one\'s idea of a marginal enthusiast, was himself a revealer, with a cycle of sealed visions to his name that Samten Karmay edited and studied. The tradition\'s centre and its edge were revealing at the same time.',
            ],
